s [Data: Relationships (21132)]. It interacts antagonistically with para-aminobenzoic acid (PABA), which can be taken up by bacteria in place of the drug, reducing efficacy [Data: Relationships (36840)]. Moreover, co-administration with bone marrow suppressants may exacerbate hematologic toxicity, necessitating regular full blood count monitoring during treatment [Data: Relationships (1888, 36847)]. These findings highlight the need for cautious prescribing in pediatric populations with comorbidities or concomitant medications.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"5-ASA drug class serves as a therapeutic bridge between sulfonamides and inflammatory bowel disease\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The 5-aminosalicylic acid (5-ASA) drug class includes olsalazine and sulfasalazine and is primarily indicated for ulcerative colitis [Data: Entities (9527, 5010); Relationships (18830)]. Olsalazine is explicitly classified under the 5-ASA category [Data: Relationships (56770)]. Sulfasalazine acts as a prodrug that is cleaved by intestinal azoreductases—produced by gut microbiota—into 5-ASA and sulfapyridine, the former providing anti-inflammatory activity [Data: Relationships (39784, 18830)]. This microbial-dependent activation links drug efficacy to individual variations in gut flora, adding a layer of complexity to treatment personalization and response prediction.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Serious adverse reactions include kernicterus, hepatotoxicity, and bleeding\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Several drugs in this network are associated with severe adverse effects. Sulfasalazine colonic-soluble formulation may displace bilirubin from plasma protein-binding sites in fetuses, posing a theoretical risk of kernicterus, though clinically evident cases are rare [Data: Relationships (12486)]. Difenidramine-sulfamethoxazole (颠茄磺苄啶) is linked to liver injury [Data: Relationships (17328)]. Meanwhile, combinations of anticoagulants, 